  "body": "## What happens\n\nA render of `main` (`4d891498e`) shows the holder form of a user holder that was made with \"Copy as user holder\" from a built-in holder. The field \"Largest diameter\" reads 50.0 mm. The drawing of the same holder shows Ø63.00 at its largest section.\n\nThe form and the drawing must show the same holder. If the field value is wrong, an Apply can write an outline that is not the holder that the user sees. The holder outline goes into the collision checks, so this is under the safety floor.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- The comparison round 2 against the tool library mockup (27 Nov 2026), screen 5 (holder editor, user copy).\n- A related observation in PR #4438: `HolderShape::of` reads the largest diameter of a test holder back as Ø38.748558 mm, not the Ø44 mm that built it, because the body sits under the flange envelope. The outline itself was identical. The form can show a value that is read back in this way.\n\n## Expected\n\n- The form shows the largest diameter of the holder outline, the same value as the drawing.\n- An Apply with no edit does not change the outline (the existing \"already so\" path).\n- An edit never makes the outline smaller than the steel that the user states (the U12 rule).\n\n## Done means\n\n- A test copies each built-in holder kind (at least one ER, one Weldon, one shrink-fit with a flange), opens it in the form model, and checks that the field value equals the largest radius × 2 of its outline.\n- A test checks that Apply with no edit keeps the outline byte for byte.\n- If the cause is in `HolderShape::of` (`crates/tools/src/holder_library_shape.rs`), the MCP tool `edit_user_holder` (PR #4438) gets the same fix or a test that shows it is not affected.\n",
